WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
A Node.js API can feel production-ready when it runs locally and passes a few Docker-based tests.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.800
The shift to Kubernetes changes the operating model.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:16.060
The application is no longer just a process started by a developer or a single container launched by hand.

4
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:28.480
It becomes a workload scheduled by a cluster, restarted by controllers, reached through Services and routing layers, configured by manifests, and observed through logs, metrics, events, and alerts.

5
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:35.300
That extra machinery is powerful, but it also exposes any weak assumptions in the application and its deployment configuration.

6
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:51.500
A common first deployment story is familiar: the container starts on a laptop, but in the cluster it fails because an environment variable is missing, a port is wrong, a database connection takes longer than expected, the readiness probe sends traffic too early, or the process exits badly during rollout.

7
00:00:51.600 --> 00:01:01.520
Kubernetes can schedule, restart, expose, scale, and replace Pods, but it depends on accurate signals from the container and clear intent in the manifests.

8
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:12.100
If the application does not start predictably, listen on the expected port, handle termination, or report health honestly, Kubernetes will automate the wrong behavior very efficiently.

9
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:18.300
For Node.js teams, production quality in Kubernetes depends on several connected decisions.

10
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.120
The image must contain the right runtime and dependencies.

11
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.180
The Deployment must describe how many copies should run and how they should be replaced.

12
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.800
Configuration and secrets must be delivered safely.

13
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.380
Resource requests and limits must match measured behavior.

14
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.640
Probes must distinguish startup, readiness, and liveness.

15
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.340
Network exposure must match the real service boundary.

16
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:49.320
Logs, metrics, traces, and events must help operators understand what the service is doing when something changes.

17
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.400
The goal is not merely to run Node in a cluster.

18
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:59.780
The goal is to operate a service that is deployable, observable, recoverable, and safe to change.

19
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.240
Kubernetes does not create operational maturity by itself.

20
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:08.700
It gives teams a vocabulary and a control plane for expressing operational intent.

21
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:17.840
This course walks through the decisions that make a Node.js service communicate that intent clearly, so releases are less surprising and incidents are easier to diagnose.
